Transient dry tickly cough secondary to post-viral airway hyperreactivity
Minor Ailments and Upsets
Non-productive prolonged cough, Tracheal tickling sensation, Exacerbation with cold air

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: MAU-043
Reason for Visit: Unremitting, exhausting cough causing significant distress and requiring assessment to rule out pneumonia, pertussis, or acute asthma.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage noted a severe, persistent cough leading to physical exhaustion. Urgent clinical assessment was mandated prior to the next in-hours period to auscultate the lung fields, assess for hypoxia or bronchospasm, and rule out lower respiratory tract infections which require prompt antibiotic or bronchodilator therapy.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: I have this constant, dry tickle in my throat that makes me cough until my ribs ache.
History of Present Illness: Patient describes a persistent, non-productive cough continuing for 2 weeks after the resolution of a mild URI. Reports an annoying tickling sensation in the laryngeal/tracheal region. Notes exacerbation of coughing upon inhaling cold air and worsening of symptoms when adopting a supine position at night. Complete absence of purulent sputum production. Denies systemic fever, weight loss, or shortness of breath at rest.
Functional Status: Fully independent, but severely sleep-deprived.
Social/Safety Concerns: Coughing fits are disrupting household sleep and causing social embarrassment.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 126/80 | HR: 76 | Temp: 36.7 C | RR: 16 | SpO2: 99% | Wt: 74 Kg
General: Alert, well-appearing, coughing intermittently in short, dry bursts.
Physical Exam: Respiratory examination reveals a normal respiratory rate without use of accessory muscles. Clear lung fields upon widespread auscultation bilaterally; no crackles, wheezes, or rhonchi. Diagnostic triad confirmed: non-productive prolonged cough, tracheal tickling sensation, and exacerbation with cold air. Normal sustained oxygen saturation on room air. Oropharynx is clear without post-nasal drip. No cervical lymphadenopathy.
Environmental Observations: Fan blowing directly onto the bed.
Medication Reconciliation: Checked against EHR. Patient taking over-the-counter cough suppressants with poor efficacy.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Transient dry tickly cough secondary to post-viral airway hyperreactivity - Status: Stable
Differentials:

Community Acquired Pneumonia - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Clear lung fields, absence of fever, and lack of purulent sputum or hypoxia.

Asthma Exacerbation - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Normal lung auscultation without wheeze, lack of chest tightness, and normal respiratory rate.

Pertussis (Whooping Cough) - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Absence of severe paroxysms followed by an inspiratory whoop or post-tussive emesis.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient's severe, prolonged cough required a direct physical evaluation to exclude serious lower respiratory pathology. Delaying the assessment until regular clinic hours posed a risk of missing early signs of consolidation or severe reactive airway disease requiring steroids. The urgent home evaluation confirmed normal oxygenation, clear lung fields, and a strictly dry, upper airway pattern, safely avoiding unnecessary antibiotic use and inappropriate ED presentation while addressing the patient's severe symptom distress.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Reassurance regarding the prolonged but benign nature of post-viral cough. Recommendation for simple linctus or honey to coat the pharynx. Short-term use of an oral first-generation antihistamine at night to suppress the cough reflex and aid sleep.
Positive Behavioral Change: Breathe strictly through the nose when outdoors or in cold air to allow the nasal turbinates to warm and humidify the air, preventing the cold-shock trigger to the hyperreactive trachea.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None indicated.
DME/Safety (Optional): Use of a bedside humidifier.
Follow-up: Routine GP review in 2 weeks if the cough has not resolved or if it becomes productive.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate presentation to Emergency Department or call 000 if you develop sudden shortness of breath, severe chest pain, cough up blood, or experience swelling of the face and lips.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis is post-viral hyperreactivity; if the cough lasts longer than 8 weeks, it requires a chronic cough workup.
Clinical Course: A post-viral dry cough can typically linger for 3 to 6 weeks. It will gradually decrease in frequency and severity.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Seek immediate medical attention if you develop a high fever, if you start coughing up dark green or brown phlegm, or if you begin wheezing.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: The virus stripped the protective lining off your windpipe, leaving the raw nerve endings exposed. Cold air or tiny dust particles trigger these nerves, causing a reflex tickle and cough. Your lungs are clear and healthy.
Actionable Steps: Sip water frequently. Use a humidifier at night. Avoid cold air. Do not demand antibiotics, as they will not heal the raw nerve endings.

